<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kakawin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as kavya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kakawin are long narrative poems in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a>, also called Kawi, written in metres derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> prosody. Poets used a formal literary language rather than the vernacular, and composed and performed the poems at the courts of central and east Javanese kings between the 9th and 16th centuries, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>. Each stanza has four lines with a fixed number of syllables arranged in patterns of heavy and light syllables, guru and laghu. Although the poems depict characters and events from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> mythology, their stories are set in the landscapes of the islands, which makes them rich sources on courtly society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and Bali.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The pattern of heavy and light syllables in a kakawin line is called its guru laghu. A syllable with a long vowel (ā, ī, ū, ö, e, o, ai and au) is always long, and so is a syllable whose vowel is followed by two consonants. The last syllable of a line may be either long or short, an anceps. In notation a long syllable is marked with a dash and a short one with U. In one metre, for example, each line has 19 syllables in a fixed sequence of long and short.</p><p>That metre opens the Kakawin Arjunawiwaha, whose first line reads ambĕk sang paramārthapaṇḍita huwus limpad sakêng śūnyatā. In a tentative English translation, its first stanza describes one who knows the Highest Knowledge, whose thought has leapt from the emptiness, who seeks not to satisfy his senses or gain worldly things but the happiness of the world, and who stands just a wayang screen away from the Mover of the World.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Dated poems include the Kakawin Śivagŗha of 856 and the Kakawin Ramayana of about 870. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-kanwa">Mpu Kanwa</a> wrote the Kakawin Arjunawiwaha around 1030, and Mpu Sedah and Mpu Panuluh the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakawin-bharatayuddha">Kakawin Bharatayuddha</a> in 1157. The Kakawin Nagarakṛtâgama, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarakretagama">Desawarnana</a>, was written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-prapanca">Mpu Prapanca</a> in 1365.</p><p>Several poets are known by more than one work. Mpu Tantular wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakawin-sutasoma">Kakawin Sutasoma</a> and the Kakawin Arjunawijaya, and Mpu Tanakung the Kakawin Siwaratrikalpa and the Kakawin Wrtasañcaya. The Kakawin Kunjarakarna is by Mpu Dusun and the Kakawin Sumanasantaka by Mpu Monaguṇa. Other notable kakawin include the Kakawin Adiparwa, the Kakawin Bhomantaka, the Kakawin Hariwangsa, the Kakawin Krsnayana and the Kakawin Smaradhana.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kakawin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in a 7th-century inscription and whose surviving text dates from the 16th century, and the Trai Bhet; the <b>Kakawin</b> Ramayana of Java, based on Bhatti's poem on the killing of Ravana rather than on Valmiki; the Balinese Ramakavaca, the Malay Hikayat Seri Rama, the Burmese Yama Zatdaw and…</blockquote><a href="/work/ramayana">Ramayana · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
